rules.requirement:
Rental housing providers may not ask about or use criminal history or criminal background checks in advertising, applications, tenant selection or decision-making. Unlawful acts toward current or prospective tenants:
- inquiring about criminal history;
- indicating that people with criminal backgrounds will not be considered;
- refusing to rent, or terminating a tenancy, based on criminal history;
- requiring disclosure of, or authorization to release, criminal history;
- demanding a higher deposit or rent based on criminal history;
- refusing to add an immediate family member based on that person's criminal history;
- disqualifying tenants from rental assistance programs such as Section 8 based on criminal history, subject to exceptions;
- treating applicants or tenants differently based on criminal history.
A landlord who takes any action based on criminal history must give the applicant or tenant written notice and an opportunity to respond. Housing providers must post the Notice to Rental Applicants and Tenants prominently on application materials, on websites and at locations under their control that applicants frequently visit. The Rent Board administers complaints.

Landlords of fully covered units may raise rent ceilings each year only by the Annual General Adjustment (AGA) that the Rent Board sets under the Rent Ordinance formula. The formula is 65% of the Bay Area CPI-U increase, approved by voters in November 2004 (BMC 13.76.110A).

Measure BB (passed November 2024):
- caps the AGA at 5% even if the formula yields more;
- still lets landlords with banked (unused) AGAs raise rent above 5% up to the rent ceiling;
- limits petitions to completed capital improvements, not planned ones;
- gives government-owned or -subsidized fully covered units a rent ceiling limited by the AGA;
- for tenancies starting on or after February 6, 2024, allows utilities to be charged in a fully covered unit only if included in base rent or separately metered in the tenant's name.

The 2026 AGA Order (Rent Board Regulation 1148, adopted October 16, 2025):
- lets eligible landlords raise 2025 permanent rent ceilings by 1.0% no earlier than January 1, 2026;
- 1.0% is 65% of the 1.5% CPI increase from July 1, 2024 to June 30, 2025;
- the AGA may not adjust rents for tenancies that began on or after January 1, 2025 whose rents were set under the Costa-Hawkins Rental Housing Act;
- Regulation 1148 sets qualifying conditions and the required tenant notice.
